description:
On 25 June 2022, Commonwealth Heads of Government adopted the ?Commonwealth Declaration on Sustainable Urbanisation? (CDSU). The Commonwealth Secretariat, alongside our development partners, are looking to develop a comprehensive work plan to support countries to implement the commitments of the CDSU, facilitate greater knowledge sharing and capacity building on urban sustainability issues, and support access to finance for resilient and sustainable cities in conjunction with achieving the Call to Action on Living Lands (CALL) and accelerate member state action on climate change.
Resources are required to develop Secretariat?s urbanisation project portfolio, engage constructively to meet member country requests, and support policy development and on-ground implementation. The CDSU specifies four key areas of action. Heads of Government will:

Acknowledge the need for integrated strategies for safe and sustainable urban development that enhance community wellbeing and security;
Support cities, municipalities, and other urban authorities to mobilise resources to develop comprehensive, scalable programmes to address key challenges of sustainable urbanisation and build climate resilience to reduce risk and vulnerability;
Encourage cities to create an enabling environment that support local economic development, job creation, and attract investment; and
Encourage sharing of knowledge and experience including through Commonwealth dialogue; twinning of cities, and increased opportunities for professional training in urban development, including town planning.

Climate Smart Agriculture in East-Africa | Climate Smart Agriculture-East Africa will promote inclusive business development for climate smart sustainable intensification in arable farming in Kenya, Tanzania and Uganda. The programme will thereby contribute to the increased availability of domestically and regionally produced food for the growing population in East Africa. As such, CSA-EA will drive systemic change through a market-driven approach that works for the rural poor. The consortium partners will leverage and create synergies with other existing programmes and plans. | Climate change in East Africa will have far-reaching consequences for agriculture, natural resources and the food security situation of the increasing number of rural and urban population. With yield drops and crop losses projected for major cash and food crops, the cost of doing nothing may be up to 20 times higher than the cost of investing in measures to adapt to climate change. This challenge demands a response that integrates enhanced food security with climate adaptation and mitigation of food crop production and supply systems, and puts greatest investment towards those interventions that lead to a market driven adoption and scaling of climate resilient agriculture through inclusive business development. | CSA-EA has 3 main impact areas:
1. Increased productive capacity for 300,000 smallholder farmers; 
2. Increased business performance for 50 agribusiness SMEs and 30 cooperatives due to climate-related investments; 
3. Climate resilient sustainable food production practiced on 600,000 hectares with improved carbon, water and energy efficiencies. | Agribusiness SMEs, cooperatives and small-holder farmers, governments, agricultural services providers, financial sector

The aim of this multi-sector project is to enhance the food security of marginalised communities in Isiolo district, Isiolo county. It will achieve this by:

•	increasing household food production through: seed provision; planting fruit trees;  training in crop production methods, animal husbandry, and savings and lending schemes;
•	improving disaster resilience through: DRR training and the formation of DRR committees; provision of energy saving jikos; conservation awareness raising; climate change advocacy; 
•	improving water access and sanitation through: rehabilitation of the water intake; provision of latrines and washing facilities in schools and homes; hygiene and sanitation training of the community and community health workers;
•	parental training and support to improve child nutrition and health;
•	inter-ethnic peace-building activities at school and community levels; 
•	staff capacity strengthening and training.

This project aims to achieve improved livelihoods security for poor women, men and children in Marsabit county, through an integrated approach that:
• increases the productivity and resilience of agricultural systems; 
• consolidates engagement in markets and diversified income generation; 
• strengthens adaptation to climate change;
• increases the ability of local, small scale food producers to access services and influence development practices. 

The targeted groups will benefit from increased crop and livestock productivity as a result of understanding and applying climate resilient agricultural practices and management of natural resources, which means they will suffer fewer losses due to climate-related shocks or environmental degradation. With increased food production and nutritional diversity at the household level, there will be year-round food security and reduced incidences of malnutrition.  They will have surplus to sell for household income and they will access fairer prices for products due to having a greater understanding of the market and how to add value, work in producer groups and engage with traders and input suppliers directly.

CAFOD Kenya and Uganda Programme launched a new programme strategy in 2023. The 2023-2030 KU strategy aspires, alongside our partners, to contribute towards resilient communities where people, particularly women, girls, and other marginalized people, thrive in a peaceful, well-governed, and healthy, supportive natural environment. Kenya-Uganda programme strategy seeks to address four key drivers of social and environmental vulnerability in fragile environments in the northern regions of Kenya and Uganda: environmental degradation, climate shocks, poor governance, and conflict.
To actualize these aspirations, the overall objective of the Needs Assessment is to assess the current situation, identify the gaps and needs of the targeted communities and recommend key interventions that meet the real needs of the targeted communities in Marsabit, Isiolo, and Samburu County and at national level for policy and advocacy across the sectors below in line with the new programme strategy. The following thematic areas among others will be addressed; Food and nutrition security, Resilience building and Livelihoods, WASH, Natural resource management, Women's Economic Empowerment, Disaster Risk Reduction and Climate Change Adaptation, Peacebuilding and good governance, Capacity Strengthening;

The findings of the needs assessment will inform the development of CAFOD and institutional-funded Programmes. By identifying and analyzing the needs across different sectors, CAFOD and Partners will develop a better understanding of the sectors that require more focus. By considering multiple sectors together, a more holistic and integrated approach and or develop specific projects, will be adopted to meet the needs of communities and populations effectively and ensure these are aligned to programme strategy.

The project aims to advance gender equality in the Commonwealth through activities that would strengthen capacity and influence policy-making and decisions of member states’ policies, frameworks and programmes to effectively promote gender equality and women’s empowerment.  The project is to support implementation of initiatives on the Commonwealth’s four priorities on Gender Equality for 2017 and beyond, i.e. (i) women’s economic empowerment; (ii) women in leadership; (iii) ending violence against women and girls; and (iv) gender and climate change, other Commonwealth commitments to gender equality within the framework of the Strategic Plan 2017/18 – 2020/21, and the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development and the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs).  The project will enable the convening of the annual consultation of the Commonwealth’s National Women’s Machineries (NWMs) during Spring or alternative dates when the United Nations convenes its Commission on the Status of Women in New York.  The consultation will focus on reporting on the member countries' progress on the four priority areas on gender equality, the Secretariat’s work on research and advocacy, capacity building, knowledge sharing, and on plans and outcomes of the Commonwealth Women’s Forum (CWF) held at margins of CHOGM, the 12th Commonwealth Women’s Affairs Ministers Meetings (WAMM) and plans for the 13th WAMM to be held in 2022.

Gender inequality perpetuates poverty and results in the vulnerability of societies due to income disparities, discriminatory laws and practices, gender stereotyping, gender discrimination and gender based violence, all of which prevent women and men from obtaining their equal social, political and economic human rights. The international commitments including the Convention on the Elimination of All forms of Discrimination Against Women (CEDAW), UNSCR 1325, the UN Beijing Platform for Action (BPfA), and regional protocols on gender equality, have not yet been fully realised.    The 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development and the SDGs provides another opportunity to advance gender equality.

Gender mainstreaming encompasses all aspects of the project cycle from planning, implementing and monitoring any social, political or economic action and can therefore be utilised as an effective tool to achieve greater gender equality and more equitable and sustainable development results. Through gender mainstreaming, greater awareness is created on how gender equality can contribute significantly to improved development outcomes.  The Secretariat through its Gender Section and other units/sections has projects and research activities that promotes women’s empowerment in a number of areas.  Some of these will be shared at the annual consultation of the NWMs, the CWF and 13th WAMM. 

This project will contribute to all the strategic outcomes of the Strategic Plan and the cross-cutting outcome: Gender mainstreaming: gender equality and the empowerment of women integrated in the Secretariat’s policies, frameworks, programmes and projects.
Through the planned project work streams, priority focus and strategic/intermediate outcomes and outputs, the Secretariat will provide evidence-based knowledge on addressing some of those persistent challenges of gender equality and share with member states.   These will include:
contributing to monitoring commitments to the Commonwealth’s four priorities for gender equality in relation to the SDGs;
advocating for non-discriminatory and gender responsive policies, laws and services in member countries; 
strengthening women’s political and economic development, particularly women’s leadership in public and political life at all levels of decision-making;
encouraging good practice, equitable systems and sustainable leadership; 
strengthening women’s access to justice focusing on violence against women;   
knowledge-sharing on economic costs of violence against women and girls;
consolidating strategic partnerships within the Commonwealth family of associations as well as intergovernmental organisations, development partners, donors, international non-governmental organisations, academic institutions, and networks to promote gender equality and particularly the recommendations from 11WAMM; and 
strengthening implementation and accountability to Commonwealth gender priorities by member states.

