In this project the Catholic Diocese of Marsabit aims to enhance food security and resilience for poor and vulnerable communities in Hula hula, Jaldesa and Bubisa locations in Marsabit county. It seeks to provide about 6000 men and women with: 
- improved income from higher crop and livestock production, achieved through training and the provision of relevant inputs;
- improved access to potable water through the construction and rehabilitation of water facilities,  training in safe hygiene and sanitation practices, and the establishment of water management committees;
- training and support for disaster preparedness and sustainable management of natural resources;
- reduced inter-community conflict, through measures to improve inter-faith and inter-community dialogue;
- identification of appropriate advocacy strategies, and training in advocacy and lobbying activities.

The project will seek to collaborate with the private sector and relevant government institutions at both at the county and national levels.

This project aims to contribute towards food security and resilience for poor and vulnerable communities in Ewasu and Torosei locations of Kajiado County, Kenya. It seeks to improve: 
• income and livestock production levels, through training in financial and business management, and training in value addition on milk products, brick making for youths, goat distribution to special groups and water bottling for youth groups;   
• community access to water for both human and livestock use and safe sanitation and hygiene practices. This will be achieved through rehabilitation of water pans, construction of sanitation facilities and through hygiene promotion activities;
• disaster management and preparedness, and sustainable management of natural resources, through training and vulnerability assessments;
• advocacy for the target communities, through the identification of key advocacy issues and appropriate training and awareness campaigns

The project will seek to collaborate with the private sector and relevant government institutions both at the county and national level.

This 18-month project supports the the diocese of Marsabit, Kenya, in its 3-year implementation of a governance and advocacy project,  using Geo Information Systems (GIS) to produce real-time Geo maps of the conflict corridors and health sector facilities within Marsabit county.  

The project has formed and trained 16 Community Education and Leadership teams (CELTs) as a community resource group to provide information and facilitate engagement as public expenditure monitors and agents of peace.  These groups monitor service delivery in the water, education, health and security sector. A county liaison desk  links the community and the county assembly on policy and legislative matters. The project will:
- familiarise stakeholders with the concept of digital mapping;
- identify and train CELT members in GIS and manual data collection;
- conduct digital and manual data collection through the trained CELT members;
- analyse the data, and share it with local communities and county government to action;
- document and share case studies with other partners. 

The project output will then be used as a tool for CELTs, communities and other actors to seek action from the county government, particularly the departments of security and health.

